    <h2>Start with evidence, not a score</h2>
    <p>Most AI tutorials show a successful prompt but leave you unable to check the result. Our examples instead preserve small inputs and explicit pass/fail conditions. You can download the fixtures, change one detail and see whether the recommended method catches it. These are <strong>not</strong> production benchmarks or verified real-world customer data.</p>
    <p>Choose one exercise below. The numbers are intentionally small so a reader can reproduce them without an AI account. We include incorrect or ambiguous conditions because a procedure that works only on perfect inputs is not much of a procedure.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-weighted-price">1. A spreadsheet average that looks right but is wrong</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> did a proposed average selling price account for how many units sold at each price? Our original fictional eight-row dataset has 27 units and revenue of 530. The revenue-weighted unit price is <strong>530 ÷ 27 = 19.629629…</strong>, displayed as 19.63. The simple average of the eight listed unit prices is <strong>153.25</strong>, a very different measurement.</p>
    <p><a href="/examples/weighted-price-sample.csv">Download the eight-row sales CSV</a> · <a href="/tools/weighted-price-checker/">Run the independent browser calculator</a> · <a href="/guides/verify-ai-weighted-average-price-spreadsheet/">Read the complete check</a>.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste the CSV into the workbench and check that Web has 7 units and revenue 280, while Store has 20 units and revenue 250.</li>
      <li>Find line L-106: its price is 999, but it sold zero units. Its contribution to sales revenue must be zero.</li>
      <li>Find line L-107: it sold five units at an actual price of zero. Blank price and zero price are <em>not</em> interchangeable.</li>
      <li>Delete the price on L-107. A reliable preflight check rejects the missing input rather than silently treating it as free.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> the calculator independently checks the original arithmetic in JavaScript. It does not reproduce a commercial accounting system, a live merchant sale or any AI model's answer.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-prompt-evaluation">2. A prompt score that must not masquerade as a benchmark</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> does a classification rule focus on the requested action rather than on a misleading word? The original eight-case teaching fixture has a target answer for each fictional support message. Two <em>editor-written</em> prediction columns have five and seven matches out of eight, respectively. They were <strong>not generated by ChatGPT or another AI service</strong>.</p>
    <p><a href="/examples/prompt-evaluation-routing.csv">Download the routing CSV</a> · <a href="/prompts/test-ai-prompts-with-evaluation-dataset/">Read the evaluation method</a>.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Compare the expected label with each illustrative prediction column: the counts should be 5/8 and 7/8.</li>
      <li>Inspect case T05: the message mentions invoices, but its requested action is restoring login access.</li>
      <li>Inspect T07: two requests have no clear priority. Under this specific teaching policy the expected label is OTHER, yet both illustrative predictions choose ACCESS. Do not hide this failure.</li>
      <li>To measure a real model, run both prompts yourself, save every raw response, record the model and date, and replace the invented example columns.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> the counts demonstrate transparent scoring, not that one prompt improves model accuracy by 25 percentage points in actual use.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-spreadsheet-filter">3. A spreadsheet filter you can verify cell by cell</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> when asked to total Paid orders in the East region, does a formula exclude West orders and unpaid East orders? Our four-row CSV contains East/Paid amounts of 120 and 30, so <strong>150</strong> is the expected sum. A West/Paid 80 and an East/Pending 50 must not be included.</p>
    <p><a href="/examples/ai-spreadsheet-sample.csv">Download the four-row CSV</a> · <a href="/examples/ai-spreadsheet-checks-results.json">Inspect our recorded Calc results</a> · <a href="/guides/how-to-use-ai-for-spreadsheet-tasks/">Follow the spreadsheet guide</a>.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Import the four rows into a disposable sheet and check the data types before using the formula.</li>
      <li>Try <code>=SUMIFS(C2:C5,A2:A5,"East",B2:B5,"Paid")</code> in a compatible locale. Compare its result with 150.</li>
      <li>Change the West amount. The East/Paid result must not change. Then change the East/Pending status to Paid; the result should rise to 200.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> the original tutorial includes a dated publisher-recorded LibreOfficeDev run. We can check the underlying sample calculation independently, but the historic app session was not rerun during the November 1 editorial audit. This is not a claim that Microsoft Excel was tested.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-document-coverage">4. A document summary must preserve the source's uncertainty</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> can a summary distinguish a refundable deposit from a total cost, and a tentative booking from a confirmed reservation? Our fictional venue policy states a capacity of 32 seated people, a request lead time of at least seven calendar days, and a refundable deposit of 50 credits. It does <em>not</em> state the rental fee or confirm that a room was reserved.</p>
    <p><a href="/examples/venue-policy-sample.txt">Read the original fictional policy</a> · <a href="/examples/document-summary-run.txt">Inspect the archived request and response</a> · <a href="/guides/how-to-summarize-long-documents-with-ai/">Read the source-check method</a>.</p>
    <p>Mark any sentence claiming that the total price is 50 credits, that a request can be confirmed tomorrow, or that the room is always available on weekends as unsupported or contradicted. These checks matter more than whether the resulting summary sounds polished.</p>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> the archived AI answer is a publisher-provided transcript; this page does not independently authenticate the historical interface session.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-source-verification">5. A source citation can still support the wrong claim</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> does blocking a page in <code>robots.txt</code> guarantee removal from Google Search? No. Crawling restrictions and index exclusion are different mechanisms. The original claim ledger marks that guarantee as contradicted and distinguishes it from the requirement that Googlebot can read a page's <code>noindex</code> instruction.</p>
    <p><a href="/examples/verification-ledger-robots.json">Inspect the source-check ledger</a> · <a href="/guides/how-to-verify-ai-generated-information/">Read the claim verification guide</a> · <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro">Read Google's robots.txt documentation</a> · <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/block-indexing">Read Google's noindex documentation</a>.</p>
    <p>This is a check against published primary guidance, not a live experiment in how quickly Google will add or remove a URL from its index.</p>
    <h3>A second source-to-answer check: policy FAQ conditions</h3>
    <p>Read the <a href="/examples/fictional-policy-faq-source.txt">nine-section fictional lending policy TXT</a> and draft answers before opening the <a href="/examples/fictional-policy-faq-reference.csv">eleven-question editorial reference CSV</a>. Every answer records a source section or a missing-evidence status. Inspect Q6: a renewal needs <strong>no competing confirmed reservation AND a request at least one calendar day before return</strong>. Inspect Q7: five late days for one kit cost $25 before the cap, but only <strong>$20 after the per-kit cap</strong>. Q10 and Q11 are not specified, not automatically false.</p>
    <p>Then change S6 in your own copy to permit renewals even with another confirmed reservation: Q6 must be reevaluated while the capped late-fee calculation and unconfirmed holiday schedule remain unchanged. The examples and answer map are original editorial teaching data, not a captured AI model run. <a href="/guides/build-policy-faq-with-ai-without-inventing-rules/">Follow the complete policy FAQ walkthrough</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-claim-boundary">6. A 22-claim audit: find which sentences are invented</h2><p><strong>Practice it directly:</strong> <a href="/tools/claim-checker/">open the interactive Source Claim Verifier</a> to read the original records, classify five or all 22 claims, and see the reasoning beside each editorial reference answer. No AI account or upload is required.</p>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> what is the difference between a statement contradicted by the source and a statement that the source simply never addresses? We wrote 22 short claims about a fictional garden plan, a software release and a proposed event. The answer key contains <strong>12 supported, 6 contradicted and 4 unsupported claims</strong>.</p>
    <p><a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">Download the original fictional notes</a> · <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-audit.csv">Download all 22 case statements and the editorial answer key</a> · <a href="/guides/what-is-generative-ai/">Start with the beginner explanation</a> · <a href="/troubleshooting/why-ai-gives-wrong-answers/">Read the failure diagnosis</a>.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the source notes first. Before looking at the answer key, write a verdict for G04, R02 and E06. Do not use a model: the goal is to check your own reading of the original.</li>
      <li>Separate <strong>UNSUPPORTED</strong> (the named garden venue has not been given) from <strong>CONTRADICTED</strong> (offline mode was not approved). Unknown is not the same as false.</li>
      <li>Reconcile the cost case: three bags at $8 and two trays at $6 give a $36 subtotal. Delivery is still unknown. Change the bag price in your own copy to $9, and recompute $39 without changing the meeting time.</li>
    </ol>
    <details><summary><strong>Reveal the checks for G04, R02 and E06</strong></summary><p><strong>G04: UNSUPPORTED.</strong> The meeting location was not confirmed. <strong>R02: CONTRADICTED.</strong> Offline mode was an unapproved proposal. <strong>E06: SUPPORTED.</strong> The event date has not been chosen. The rest of the 22-case reference key is included in the downloadable CSV.</p></details>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> this is an original, independently inspectable editorial exercise. The cases, source notes and key were authored for this site. The <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/tests/claim-boundary-audit.test.mjs">open integrity test</a> checks their completeness and sample arithmetic. <strong>No model was run to create these verdicts</strong>; there is no live model accuracy rate to infer.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-observed-browser">7. What happened when we used the real website</h2>
    <p><strong>November 1, 2026 — direct browser use, not an invented transcript.</strong> We opened our live <a href="/tools/weighted-price-checker/">Weighted Average Audit Workbench</a> in a real browser, operated its buttons, changed the sample CSV and read the visible results. The input was fictional, but the interface interactions and outcomes were <strong>actually observed</strong>.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Browser exercise</th><th>Observed output</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Original eight rows</td><td>27 units, 530 revenue, weighted price 19.63; simple row-price mean 153.25</td></tr>
    <tr><td>L-102 quantity changed from 4 to 40</td><td>63 units, 1,430 revenue, weighted price 22.70; row-price mean stayed 153.25</td></tr>
    <tr><td>L-107 unit price replaced with a blank</td><td>Precise blank-price error; previous successful tables and totals disappeared rather than remaining stale</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>The relevant files are the <a href="/examples/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.json">structured browser observation ledger</a>, the <a href="/examples/weighted-price-sample.csv">exact fictional CSV</a> and the <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/docs/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.md">dated testing procedure</a>. You can use them to repeat the same actions. The <a href="/guides/verify-ai-weighted-average-price-spreadsheet/">worked article</a> now distinguishes these direct observations from source-derived spreadsheet advice.</p>
    <p><strong>An unresolved observation was also recorded, not concealed:</strong> an automated attempt at five <a href="/tools/claim-checker/">Source Claim Verifier</a> questions displayed 1/5 even though the written automation plan had four reference-matching selections. We did <strong>not</strong> capture the actual checked radio states before submitting, so we cannot assign the discrepancy to scoring or selection. The source scoring logic's five intended answers give 4/5 in a separate deterministic test. In response, we made the selection count visible, gave each option a unique accessible label, and blocked grading until all cases are answered. The initial 1/5 run remains <strong>inconclusive</strong>, but a separate <a href="https://agent.tinyfish.ai/runs/ff9a4360-9346-42d2-8d0d-bcf09beeb690">post-fix live-browser smoke test</a> confirmed that selecting only one of five answers displays the selection count and <strong>blocks grading without showing a misleading score</strong>. A separate <a href="https://agent.tinyfish.ai/runs/e6804798-a2cd-4872-9283-a2c5761d5391">complete five-answer production-browser test</a> subsequently inspected all five checked inputs before scoring and observed the expected <strong>4/5</strong>, with the intentionally wrong G04 as the only mismatch. That verifies the current quiz path but does not explain the earlier run's unrecorded selections. The exact case IDs, input labels and observations are preserved in the <a href="/examples/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.json">public evidence ledger</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>Limits:</strong> this is real use of GuideHollow's own published browser tools. It is not a ChatGPT, Excel, Google Sheets or customer case study, and it is not a universal success rate or controlled cross-browser comparison. No actual commercial transaction or AI benchmark was conducted.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-prompt-edit">8. An actual prompt edit—and a stale-export trap</h2>
    <p><strong>Real browser interaction, November 1, 2026:</strong> we opened our own <a href="/tools/ai-prompt-builder/">AI Prompt Builder</a>, loaded the fictional garden example, generated a prompt, and manually changed its output from <code>under 70 words</code> to <code>under 50 words</code>. The edited text appeared in the live output field; the previous phrase was absent. The Copy button reported success, but we <strong>did not independently inspect the clipboard contents</strong>.</p>
    <p>Next we changed the input audience to <code>Garden volunteers</code>. The previous edited draft stayed visible, but <strong>Copy and Download TXT were disabled</strong> until we rebuilt the prompt. The rebuilt output included the new audience; Reset cleared the source fields and output and disabled both export actions. That is a direct test of whether changing the source can lead a visitor to use outdated instructions. It is not a model-accuracy experiment.</p>
    <p>Read the <a href="https://agent.tinyfish.ai/runs/46b0221c-85d6-4b67-9d0c-45b0e0367fa9">actual public browser run</a>, inspect the <a href="/examples/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.json">precise observations and limits</a>, or <a href="/tools/ai-prompt-builder/">reproduce the experiment yourself</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-notebook-packet">9. A Gemini Notebook source packet you can actually upload</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> can you tell which claim comes from which source without mixing a walkthrough location with a separate practice session? We published three original, fictional UTF-8 source files to accompany our <a href="/ai-tools/notebooklm-source-checked-study-guide/">Gemini Notebook study-guide tutorial</a>. The exercise does not require a Google account to check the reference claims.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Download the <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/exhibit-overview-v1.txt">exhibit overview</a>, <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/training-agenda-v2.txt">current training agenda</a>, and <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/visitor-questions-v1.txt">visitor-question notes</a>. Read the files in a text editor or import them as separate notebook sources.</li>
      <li>Use the <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/claim-audit-worksheet.tsv">ten-claim practice worksheet</a> to classify each statement before opening the <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/claim-audit-answer-key.tsv">editorial answer key</a>. The key contains three supported, four contradicted and three not-established claims.</li>
      <li>For a more difficult source-selection check, inspect the <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/source-register.tsv">fictional revision register</a> and optionally add the <a href="/examples/gemini-notebook/training-agenda-v1-superseded.txt">superseded agenda</a>. Confirm that a rejected old draft cannot quietly replace the selected v2 details.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> these downloadable documents and answer classifications were written by GuideHollow Editorial. They are not a real museum data set, proof of a Gemini Notebook model run, a product benchmark, or evidence that any third-party account was accessed. The <a href="/ai-tools/notebooklm-source-checked-study-guide/">guide</a> explains how to reproduce and audit the source-grounded task in your own environment.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-json-fidelity">10. When valid JSON still invents a support ticket result</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> can a two-ticket JSON output pass parsing and structural checks but still contradict the original fictional source? Our <a href="/guides/ai-valid-json-without-inventing-data/">JSON extraction guide</a> now includes ten deliberately varied candidates, a separate answer key and a dependency-free Node.js script for repeatable checks.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the <a href="/examples/json-fidelity/source-notes.txt">fictional support notes</a>, <a href="/examples/json-fidelity/reference.json">editor-written reference JSON</a> and <a href="/examples/json-fidelity/ticket-schema.json">two-ticket structure contract</a>.</li>
      <li>Classify the <a href="/examples/json-fidelity/candidate-outputs.json">ten candidates</a> yourself as PASS, SYNTAX, STRUCTURE or SOURCE, then compare with the <a href="/examples/json-fidelity/answer-key.tsv">independent editorial answer key</a>.</li>
      <li>Download the <a href="/examples/json-fidelity/audit.mjs">offline audit script</a> into the same folder and run <code>node audit.mjs</code> to reproduce the expected 1 pass, 1 syntax failure, 4 structural failures and 4 source mismatches.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>What it proves:</strong> only that the published fixed dataset and script distinguish these three classes under the disclosed, simplified rules. It is not a universal JSON Schema validation library, real customer data, an AI-model benchmark or a test of a live third-party service.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-pdf-ocr">11. Two PDFs that look alike but extract differently</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> why can an AI PDF summary omit a visibly legible table? Start by distinguishing a selectable text layer from a page image. Our <a href="/troubleshooting/ai-pdf-summary-missing-tables-ocr-check/">PDF extraction guide</a> provides two actual original one-page files containing the same fictional expenses and a separate, inspectable generation method.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/examples/pdf-ocr-fixtures/fictional-expense-selectable-text.pdf">Download the text-layer PDF</a> and <a href="/examples/pdf-ocr-fixtures/fictional-expense-image-only.pdf">download the image-only PDF</a>. Both display January $120 + $80 = $200, February $95 + $105 = $200, and a $400 grand total.</li>
      <li>Try selecting <strong>Feb 2026</strong> in each PDF. The image-only version contains a page image rather than an ordinary searchable text layer. Some viewers can apply their own OCR; this is a different operation from basic text extraction.</li>
      <li>Inspect the <a href="/examples/pdf-ocr-fixtures/manifest.json">generated-file manifest</a> for the reproducible expected values. In our local PyMuPDF 1.26.7 check, the selectable file yielded <strong>263 characters</strong>, and the raster-only file <strong>0 characters</strong> without OCR.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> the source PDFs were generated from a <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/scripts/generate-pdf-ocr-fixtures.mjs">public dependency-free Node script</a>. These results compare ordinary local PDF text extraction only. They do not measure OCR quality, AI model performance, customer documents, or ChatGPT/Adobe/Google Drive uploads.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-csv-source-integrity">12. Download the complete CSV—then make one row fail</h2>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> can an AI-assisted spreadsheet workflow reconcile the <em>same source file</em> that its tutorial describes? Two original fictional CSV inputs are now directly downloadable, not only shown as copyable code snippets. The site checks that each downloaded file is byte-for-byte consistent with the article's published source block and that the fixed totals are reproducible.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/examples/csv-duplicate-orders-fictional.csv">Download nine fictional order records</a> for the <a href="/guides/audit-ai-csv-duplicate-removal-without-losing-rows/">duplicate-removal guide</a>. The raw Amount sum is <strong>490</strong>. One repeated O-102 row worth 80 may be excluded after checking its four fields; the retained eight rows sum to <strong>410</strong>. A conflicting O-103 ID and a missing ID still require review.</li>
      <li><a href="/examples/monthly-timestamp-orders-fictional.csv">Download ten fictional timestamped orders</a> for the <a href="/guides/audit-ai-monthly-totals-with-timestamps/">monthly-boundary guide</a>. October records with Status Paid total <strong>295</strong>, when using the half-open date interval from October 1 to November 1. A flawed upper boundary at October 31 midnight gives <strong>190</strong>, missing later October 31 Paid records.</li>
      <li><strong>Try a failure:</strong> in a disposable copy of the duplicates file, change O-102's second amount from 80 to 81. It should no longer be safe to remove a same-ID row without reconciliation. In the monthly file, shift R-10 from October 31 at 23:59 to November 1 at 00:00; the expected October Paid total falls from 295 to <strong>270</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> these are complete editor-created input records, and the described sums, row counts, flags and boundary changes are independently checked in automated tests. We have <strong>not</strong> tested a live AI model, an Excel spreadsheet session, a Google Sheets session, or a merchant's actual orders. Real financial records require an approved source, a defined business timezone and human review.</p>

    <h2 id="evidence-limitations">What we checked, and what remains unproven</h2>
    <p>Our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> distinguishes checked calculations, source comparisons, fabricated examples and recorded model responses. The <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/docs/editorial-fact-check-2026-11-01.md">31-article audit register</a> describes the scope of our November 1 review and its unresolved limitations. The related <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/tests/editorial-fact-audit.test.mjs">test file</a> independently recalculates example figures and verifies that published articles have structured source references.</p>
    <p>We have not independently repeated every historic ChatGPT run, tested every spreadsheet product version, or proven a universal AI performance claim. The purpose of these exercises is to help readers <strong>detect mistakes themselves</strong>, not to make a small example look like large-scale research.</p>
    <p>Found a contradiction, broken download or missing condition? <a href="/contact/">Report it to GuideHollow</a>, including the relevant example and the result you obtained.</p>
